  "worked_solution": "Notice that as $t\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3t-6})}^{t-2}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nIn the given limit, we let $3t-6=n.$ Thus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3t-6 & =n \\\\ 3(t-2) & =n \\\\ t-2 & =\\frac{n}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3t-6})}^{t-2} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={e}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
